notes: Hypothesis partly confirmed. N axis (t=0..120 per stage): both sides scale linearly in N (fitted exponents 1.07 / 1.01); for N>=16 the WITHOUT median frame costs 53-65x the WITH median frame (at N=16384 the mean-frame ratio is 24x and the attempted-solver-step ratio 24x), because >99% of WITHOUT towns collapse (Wood below 1e-6, Food and Workers past 1e6, halted at 1e12) and the stiff collapse forces many small steps, while WITH towns settle and take about one step per frame. WITHOUT crosses 16.7 ms between N=1024 (12.4 ms) and N=4096 (46.3 ms); WITH stays under 16.7 ms up to N=16384. WITH-only extension: N=65536 gives 16.54 ms median. Time-horizon axis (N=256): the extra cost is a transient, not growth with simulated time. With the 01-economy halt rule WITHOUT costs 53x WITH per unit time in t=(10,100], then less than WITH once its towns are halted (0.57x in (100,1000], about 0 later). Never halting: 144x in (10,100], 63x in (100,1000], 0.91x in (1000,1e4] (collapsed towns sit at extreme but slowly varying values that the relative-error controller steps through as cheaply as WITH). Fixed-step explicit midpoint (what a game ships; same cost per step on both sides by construction): at every step from h=0.5 to h=1/512 at least 99.6% of WITHOUT towns go negative or non-finite by t=1000 (99.2% after t=10 at h=1/512); WITH towns break only during the start transient from extreme starts (2.0% after t=10 at h=0.5, none after t=10 for h<=0.125, 0.0% in total at h=1/512). Proved by the paper (thm:main, eq:uniform-bounds): WITH trajectories stay in [eps,1/eps] after a transient; eps is not computed. Everything else here (costs, the WITHOUT collapse, fixed-step behaviour) is measured for this network, these seeds and rates, 1 thread per side.
